I keep getting confused with the parameter format. Let me be very explicit. The execute function takes `request` as a parameter. Looking at the schemas:

```json
{
  "execute", 
  "parameters": {
    "properties": {
      "request": {
        "description": "inline request content (REST: `{method, url, headers?, body?, protocol: \"rest\"}`; GraphQL: `{protocol: \"graphql\", query, variables?}`; MCP: `{protocol: \"mcp\", tool, arguments?}`). Provide `target` OR `request`, not both.",
      },
      "target": ...
    }
  }
}
```

The request needs to be passed as a JSON object. Let me try a different approach and write the request as an entity then execute it by target.

I keep passing empty objects. Let me very carefully construct the JSON object. Perhaps the issue is my JSON object format. Let me look at what worked before — I had `{"protocol": "rest", "method":"POST", "url":"http://localhost:9001/v3/oauth/token","headers":{"Content-Type":"application/x-www-form-urlencoded"},"body":{"client_id":"{{client_id}}","client_secret":"{{client_secret}}","grant_type":"{{grant_type}}"}}`. That worked once. Let me do it carefully:

OK looking at the schema:
- The named parameter for execute is `request`. So I should write:
```json
{
  "execute": {
    "request": {...}
  }
}
```

For write:
```json
{
  "write": {
    "entity_ref": {"kind":"request","id":"oauth-token"},
    "content": {...}
  }
}
```

I think the issue is the old system accepted both object and stringified JSON. Let me just try execute with clearly correct JSON object.
